  {
    "episode_id": 72856,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72856.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "Brooklyn swung extraordinarily towards Donald Trump. So it's really, really important that we not try to say, slice and dice the electorate to the point where we are overlooking where Trump voters are. And actually, the four boroughs besides Staten Island all shifted towards Donald Trump by five to 12 percentage points. Now, what's interesting about this race, there's this notion that, well, all politics are local, all politics have become nationalized. Actually, what happened in my race and in many parts of New York City and actually in Long Island too. is that it became both a local and nationalized race. Donald Trump is a hometown hero. He played a particular focus or paid a particular focus to some of these congressional districts, particularly mine. But this was also a district or a politics that spoke quite a lot about Bill de Blasio. Governor Cuomo's shutdowns were not popular at all. And so everything kind of came to the forefront. And then, of course, I represent probably more police officers than any other district in America. And this played a very significant role. So, amongst the major factors here that I think you see in my race. The first is is Donald Trump is on the belt and that just generally causes a surge of Donald Trump supporters who are going straight down the line. Notice I'm not saying Republicans because Donald Trump supporters are not Republicans. The second thing and this has been totally lost in the analysis across the country is impeachment. I ran and I still believe that I am not the person who blindly wields partisan pitchforks, but I did make the decision to vote for impeachment. It's a decision I would make again knowing that I was risking my political future, or perhaps signing its debt warrant. But to many, to some people, that was viewed as a partisan act. You sided with the Dems. You buckled to do this. And then the third was my participation in a And again, something I would do again in a Black Lives Matter's march to a local police precinct that was completely peaceful, organized by young leaders on Staten Island, mostly young people of color, under the banner of calling for peace and justice for all so that they could be heard. They did this completely in the right way, organized with the police. But nonetheless, they were millions of dollars in commercials came thereafter. saying, Matt Rose marched with them, the rioters, the looters, the defund crowd. Now, that's politics fine, come at me. But the fact that they came at young people in my district as well who were trying to do the right thing and positioned them as criminals and violent criminals at that, that to me is absolutely evil. And that's an edifice that I'm going to continue to try to make more against. But let me just close up by saying this. When you win an election, man, everyone thinks you're a genius. No one ever does a conversation like this after you win an election. They just think you got it all right, you look perfect, and people act like they're perfect. When you lose an election, everyone kind of jumps in and dissects it and talks about ways in which you failed. It feels awfully like what I imagine it would be if you're lying in an open casket and you're friends and family are debating your death. But what I will not allow for is for people to look at my loss and say that that should warrant a retreat from issues. Oh, Maxwell's lost. So now we have to retreat from criminal justice reform. Maxwell's lost. So now we have to retreat from addressing racial inequities, socioeconomic inequities, housing inequities, and the like, and having that as a lens by which we address and view policymaking. My name was on the ballot. I take responsibility for this.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
