    "turn_text": "know, Maxwell's turned against us. He turns against the men and women and uniform. And we need to be Understand where both sides are coming from here. That's not to say that emotions are equal, that's not to say fears are equal, their anxieties are equal. But from the perspective of many police officers, what they are thinking is, and look, I felt a very similar sentiment in Afghanistan. It's like, look, man, I go outside every day. I don't know if I'm coming back. I'm under constant threat, constant danger. And I'm living with this consistent anxiety that if I do something wrong, if I have a bad day, if I make a mistake, the entire world is going to come crashing down. I mean, people will presume my guilt rather than my innocence. Politicians are going to jump on the bandwagon, then the marches are going to come up, this and that. that there's definitely a thread of that. Conversely though, when we're looking at protests and this emerging movement, I think that there's an absence of an understanding that yes, this is about the incident at hand, but this is also about a legacy of injustice. It's about a legacy of court-based policing. It's about a legacy of housing inequities. It's about the list goes on and on and on of a legacy in reality of racism. And it's for that reason, above all else, and I think it was so vital for me to be there in this march. Because it was important, it was my responsibility as their member of Congress. And I made a pledge to represent every single community, to listen, to be there, to show my respect and to show my commitment to trying to ensure a more racially just American. And that division, that conflict, I think really came to the forefront in my race. I'm a guy who I consider myself pro cop. I consider myself like, I want to see police officers well resourced. I think they need to be paid as the heroes that they are. But I don't think that that's in conflict with me also saying that I think that police officers, when they step over the line, should be held accountable. And have to be a ability mechanism is in place. And also, we should not have a form of policing in America that criminalizes human behavior in certain communities. And it is the responsibility of all of us to build that system, to build the America we know as possible. But nonetheless, though, this was subject to an age old form of politics. And I was thrown right into the middle of it and unable to fight my way out of it. But I don't regret doing the thing. Well,",
