    "turn_text": "if it can't be salvaged, what you have to do is you have to just sap it of funds to make sure it doesn't have power. That's the argument, yes. That's something I fundamentally disagree with. In fact, I think, like, look, If you had a racist and prejudicial police department, and then you reduced it's budget by 50%, do you think suddenly it's not racist and prejudicial? Like the argument, I don't think carries forward, but we do have a problem in America. And I think that this goes back to CompStat-based policing, where if you are going to concentrate police officers in areas of high crime, which is the right thing to do, and then you're going to also have a statistics-based form of policing that is tied to quotas or implicit-based quotas, what you are doing is you're criminalizing human behavior. And so there needs to be an effort And then there's a myriad of examples about what needs to be improved, but I'm not gonna give up on our public institutions. I think that we should still, we should be thinking about how do we invest in them more across the board? And how do we try to lead culture changes, accountability changes, strategic changes, so as to build the public institutions that we know are possible.",
