    "turn_idx": 70,
    "turn_text": "And it's like, and I respect the deathliness of this disease, but it's gotten a little much. People's businesses, when you're a small business owner, when you're a restaurant owner, that's your life, is just withering right in front of them. And with these shutdowns and in the absence of additional federal aid, there was the perception that people were not showing sensitivity towards that. We weren't showing a balancing of risk about that. Now, we had a stand-on in front of Max Public House, recently a gigantic gathering of folks protesting the fact that it had been shut down due to flouting the rules. Now, what's fascinating about this is that the owner or someone who worked there got arrested for nearly running over or for running over and injuring a law enforcement official. At one point in this, people who started chanting defund the sheriff's, which I just felt freaking ironic. It's really amazing. But what's interesting here is that So you have, in the span of four months on Staten Island, you have one march for racial justice, marching to a police precinct. You have another massive protest against shutdowns, one of which does not have violence, the Black Lives Matter's march, the other of which does have violence, and the Black Lives Matter's march is vilified as the rioters, the looters, and the other gathering is not. And what I will not do is try to disrespect, and they, by the way, had this gathering, they had the Proud Boys there, and this and that, I'm not about to disrespect the pain and the suffering that our small businesses are going through. It is pain at an unbelievable level, just because there were acts of violence and a demonstration in support of them. I refuse to do that one wrong or two longs don't make a right here. But it is we there were certain elements of the liberal establishment. that did not necessarily show sensitivity towards what was happening. On the same hand, though, you had Mr. the Republicans standing in the way.",
