  {
    "episode_id": 72856,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72856.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 71,
    "turn_text": "That's the thing. I mean, the thing I find maddening about this is that I'll give you my take as someone who, what the hell do I know? I don't have to actually face voters. I think, basically, they arrived early on, Trump and Republicans, that they were going to declare bankruptcy on actually trying to deal with the pandemic. I mean, they really did. They basically said, we're not actually going to try to have a test and trace and suppression and all these things other countries has done. And then, politically, we're going to try to drive the wedge and polarize on, do you want to be locked down or do you want to be back to normal? And by abdicating their responsibility and by McConnell and others abdicating responsibility, it was a fairly effective wedge because of the idea as well, do you want your business to close or not? A lot of people are like, well, I don't want my business to close. But it was always a completely false choice that they created. And it drove me insane to watch it happen and to watch it work to a certain",
