  {
    "episode_id": 72856,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72856.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 100,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, look, I certainly was proud of my record. I'm proud to have gotten legislation done to initiate the seawall project on these shores that now, which is going to be the largest resiliency project in the history of New York City. Proud that we took thousands of trucks off the road was split to only on the Arizona bridge. local things, proud of some international things. We did like sanctions on global pharmaceutical companies who are producing illicit fentanyl that's killing kids in my congressional district. And throughout the country, proud we led the effort to increase funding massively for the fight against the opioid epidemic. Proud that we're leading the way to overturn the Muslim ban. But, you know, In the end, though, it did go our way. And I'll probably spend a good portion of the rest of my life thinking about that. And I'm sure depending on the year, there will be a different analysis. And sometimes you just lose elections. It's just the way it goes and there was nothing you could do or there was something you could do and we'll have to try to figure that out. But what I can say is like, and this is not I'm not trying to segue to something about me, but I can say that I'm not so disheartened by the electoral outcome of my race that I am permanently turned off from public service. like I want to stay in the fight. And it remains to be seen how that happens. But there's many ways, there's plenty of ways that happen and history does show us that if you just keep on insisting that you want to help, eventually you find a way to help.",
